Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• companies_funding.company_id : join key  
• companies_funding.valuation : filter for “unicorns” ( >= 1 000 000 000 USD )  

• companies_dates.company_id : join key  
• companies_dates.date_joined : determine the calendar year a company first appeared (2019‑2021 window)  

• companies_industries.company_id : join key  
• companies_industries.industry : group results by industry to find the “top” (most unicorns 2019‑2021)  

Typical conditions to apply  

1. companies_funding.valuation >= 1000000000  
2. substr(companies_dates.date_joined,1,4) BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (LIMIT 20 where sensible) that move from simple inspection to the final step of computing the requested average.  
Each query is annotated so you can see why it is run.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the raw valuation figures
SELECT company_id, valuation
FROM companies_funding
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List distinct valuations >= 1 B to verify the unicorn filter
SELECT DISTINCT valuation
FROM companies_funding
WHERE valuation >= 1000000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Make sure date strings look consistent (first 20 rows)
SELECT company_id, date_joined
FROM companies_dates
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Extract the 4‑digit year from date_joined and preview the 2019‑2021 window
SELECT company_id,
       substr(date_joined,1,4) AS yr
FROM companies_dates
WHERE yr BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Quick look at industries represented in the data
SELECT DISTINCT industry
FROM companies_industries
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Find all unicorns created in 2019‑2021 (show a sample of 20)
SELECT f.company_id,
       substr(d.date_joined,1,4) AS yr,
       i.industry
FROM   companies_funding     AS f
JOIN   companies_dates       AS d ON d.company_id = f.company_id
JOIN   companies_industries  AS i ON i.company_id = f.company_id
WHERE  f.valuation >= 1000000000
  AND  substr(d.date_joined,1,4) BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Count unicorns per industry for the 2019‑2021 window
SELECT i.industry,
       COUNT(*) AS unicorn_cnt
FROM   companies_funding     AS f
JOIN   companies_dates       AS d ON d.company_id = f.company_id
JOIN   companies_industries  AS i ON i.company_id = f.company_id
WHERE  f.valuation >= 1000000000
  AND  substr(d.date_joined,1,4) BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
GROUP  BY i.industry
ORDER  BY unicorn_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as #7 but also broken out by year, to see the distribution
SELECT substr(d.date_joined,1,4)  AS yr,
       i.industry,
       COUNT(*)                   AS unicorn_cnt
FROM   companies_funding     AS f
JOIN   companies_dates       AS d ON d.company_id = f.company_id
JOIN   companies_industries  AS i ON i.company_id = f.company_id
WHERE  f.valuation >= 1000000000
  AND  yr BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
GROUP  BY yr, i.industry
ORDER  BY yr, unicorn_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Determine the single “top industry” (highest total unicorns 2019‑21)
SELECT i.industry
FROM   companies_funding     AS f
JOIN   companies_dates       AS d ON d.company_id = f.company_id
JOIN   companies_industries  AS i ON i.company_id = f.company_id
WHERE  f.valuation >= 1000000000
  AND  substr(d.date_joined,1,4) BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
GROUP  BY i.industry
ORDER  BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: average # of new unicorns per year in that top industry (2019‑21)
WITH top_ind AS (
  SELECT i.industry
  FROM   companies_funding     AS f
  JOIN   companies_dates       AS d ON d.company_id = f.company_id
  JOIN   companies_industries  AS i ON i.company_id = f.company_id
  WHERE  f.valuation >= 1000000000
    AND  substr(d.date_joined,1,4) BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
  GROUP  BY i.industry
  ORDER  BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 1
),
yr_counts AS (
  SELECT substr(d.date_joined,1,4) AS yr,
         COUNT(*)                  AS unicorn_cnt
  FROM   companies_funding     AS f
  JOIN   companies_dates       AS d ON d.company_id = f.company_id
  JOIN   companies_industries  AS i ON i.company_id = f.company_id
  JOIN   top_ind               AS t ON t.industry = i.industry
  WHERE  f.valuation >= 1000000000
    AND  yr BETWEEN '2019' AND '2021'
  GROUP  BY yr
)
SELECT ROUND(AVG(unicorn_cnt),2) AS avg_unicorns_per_year
FROM   yr_counts;
```

